    body: `<p>In the opening chapters of Genesis, God places the first humans in a garden and gives them a task: tend it. The Hebrew word is <em>shamar</em> — to keep, to guard, to watch over. The human relationship to the earth, in the Bible's own framing, is not ownership but stewardship. Not dominion in the sense of domination but care in the sense of guardianship. The earth belongs to God. Humans are its caretakers, charged with preserving what they have been given.</p>

<p>This theology is clear in the text. It is almost entirely absent from the practice of Western Christianity. The Christian West — shaped by the Enlightenment, the Industrial Revolution, and the capitalist logic of extraction and consumption — has treated the earth not as a garden to be tended but as a resource to be exploited. Forests have been clear-cut, rivers poisoned, species driven to extinction, and the climate itself destabilized — all within cultures that claimed the Bible as their foundational text. The disconnect between the theology and the practice is staggering.</p>

<p>Indigenous spiritual traditions, across the globe and across their enormous diversity, have preserved what Western Christianity forgot: that the earth is sacred, that humans are part of the creation rather than above it, that the land has a voice and the creatures have a dignity that human ambition does not override. The church that destroyed these traditions in the name of evangelization silenced the very voices that were saying what the Bible itself says. The irony is painful. The cost is incalculable.</p>

<h2>Creation Care: What the West Forgot</h2>

<p>Indigenous traditions understand the human relationship to the earth in terms that Western Christianity, for all its theological resources, has largely failed to practice. The Lakota concept of <em>Mitakuye Oyasin</em> — "all my relatives" — expresses a relational understanding of the created order in which humans, animals, plants, rivers, mountains, and the earth itself are bound together in a web of kinship. This is not animism in the crude sense that Western missionaries attributed to it — the worship of natural objects. It is a sophisticated ecological theology that recognizes what the Bible affirms and Western practice denies: that creation is a community, not a commodity.</p>

<p>The Haudenosaunee (Iroquois) Thanksgiving Address — recited at the opening of gatherings — names and thanks every element of the created order: the earth, the waters, the fish, the plants, the trees, the animals, the birds, the winds, the sun, the moon, the stars. It is a liturgy of gratitude that takes twenty minutes to recite because it refuses to take any element of creation for granted. It names each gift individually, acknowledges its contribution to the whole, and expresses the community's dependence on and gratitude for what has been given.</p>

<p>Compare this with the average Christian prayer before a meal — a perfunctory few seconds that names God as the source of blessing but says nothing about the earth that produced the food, the animals that gave their lives, the water that nourished the crops, or the human labor that brought the meal to the table. The Haudenosaunee Thanksgiving Address practices, in concrete liturgical form, the theology of creation care that the Bible teaches but the church has reduced to an abstraction.</p>

<p>Wendell Berry — a Christian farmer, poet, and essayist — has spent a lifetime arguing that the church's failure of creation care is not a peripheral issue but a theological crisis. "The ecological teaching of the Bible is simply inescapable," he writes. "God made the world because He wanted it made. He thinks the world is good, and He loves it. It is His world; He has never relinquished title to it." A theology that treats the earth as disposable, Berry argues, has not understood the God who made it. Indigenous traditions that treat the earth as sacred have understood something that the Christian West, for all its theological sophistication, has not.</p>

<h2>Community Over Individualism</h2>

<p>Western Christianity, particularly in its Protestant and evangelical forms, has become radically individualized. Salvation is a personal decision. Faith is a private relationship with God. The spiritual life is conceived primarily in individual terms: my quiet time, my prayer life, my relationship with Jesus. The community exists to support the individual's spiritual formation, not the other way around.</p>

<p>Indigenous traditions operate on fundamentally different assumptions. The individual exists within and through the community. Identity is communal before it is personal. Decisions are made collectively. Spiritual practices are communal practices. The self is understood not as an autonomous agent pursuing its own spiritual goals but as a node in a web of relationships — with other humans, with ancestors, with the land, with the spirit world — that constitute the self's identity and meaning.</p>

<p>This communal orientation is not foreign to Christianity. It is, in fact, deeply biblical. The New Testament's dominant metaphor for the church is the body — a single organism composed of interdependent members, none of which can say to the others, "I have no need of you." The early church held all things in common. The Hebrew concept of corporate identity — in which the individual is constituted by the community rather than the community by individuals — runs through the entire Old Testament. The Western turn toward individualism is not a biblical development. It is an Enlightenment development that Christianity absorbed uncritically.</p>

<p>Indigenous traditions can help the church recover what the Enlightenment displaced: the understanding that the spiritual life is inherently communal, that the self is formed in relationship, and that the radical individualism of contemporary Christianity is not a feature of the faith but a distortion of it.</p>

<h2>The Sacred in the Ordinary</h2>

<p>One of the most significant features of indigenous spiritual traditions is the refusal to separate the sacred from the ordinary. There is no division between the spiritual and the material, the holy and the everyday, the place where God is present and the place where God is not. The entire created order is pervaded with spiritual significance. A meal is a ceremony. A walk is a meditation. The land is not the backdrop for spiritual life. It is the medium of spiritual life.</p>

<p>Christianity, in its Western form, has been deeply shaped by the Greek dualism that separates matter and spirit — a dualism that is foreign to the Hebrew worldview in which Christianity is rooted. The sacred has been confined to sacred spaces (churches), sacred times (Sundays), sacred activities (worship), and sacred personnel (clergy). The ordinary — work, eating, walking, planting, resting — has been relegated to the secular, the non-spiritual, the domain of the merely material.</p>

<p>Indigenous traditions know better. And so, it should be noted, does the Bible. The Psalms find God in the mountains, the seas, the storms, and the stars. The incarnation — God becoming flesh, dwelling among us — is the ultimate refusal of the sacred-secular divide. A God who became a carpenter, ate meals with sinners, touched lepers, and washed feet has declared, in the most definitive way possible, that the ordinary is the dwelling place of the holy.</p>

<p>The recovery of this understanding — the recognition that God is present not only in the sanctuary but in the field, not only in the sermon but in the silence, not only in the church building but in the created order that surrounds and sustains it — is one of the most important gifts indigenous spirituality has to offer the Christian tradition. It is also one of the most faithful, because it recovers what the Bible teaches and the church has lost.</p>

<h2>The Damage Done in Christ's Name</h2>

<p>Any honest engagement between Christianity and indigenous spirituality must begin with an acknowledgment of the damage the church has done. The destruction of indigenous spiritual traditions was not an unfortunate side effect of well-intentioned mission work. It was a deliberate strategy, theologically justified and institutionally implemented. Missionaries and colonial administrators classified indigenous spiritual practices as paganism, devil worship, or superstition — and then systematically destroyed them. Ceremonies were banned. Sacred sites were desecrated. Spiritual leaders were imprisoned or killed. Children were removed from their families and placed in institutions designed to erase their cultural and spiritual identity.</p>

<p>The theological justification for this destruction rested on assumptions that the church must now name and repudiate. The assumption that God was entirely absent from indigenous traditions before the missionaries arrived. The assumption that indigenous spiritual practices were uniformly demonic. The assumption that cultural destruction was a necessary precondition for spiritual salvation. These assumptions are not merely wrong. They are blasphemous — attributing to God the absence of the very creative and sustaining work that Scripture says God performs among all peoples and in all creation.</p>

<h2>Richard Twiss and Indigenous Christian Theology</h2>

<p>Richard Twiss spent his life demonstrating that it was possible to be both fully indigenous and fully Christian — that the forced choice between the two was a colonial imposition, not a theological necessity. A member of the Rosebud Sioux tribe and an ordained minister, Twiss advocated for indigenous expressions of Christian worship that drew on native cultural forms — drumming, dance, regalia, ceremony — as legitimate vehicles for encountering the Christian God.</p>

<p>Twiss's work was controversial within both indigenous and Christian communities. Some indigenous people viewed him as a collaborator with the religion that had destroyed their ancestors' traditions. Some Christians viewed his incorporation of indigenous cultural forms as syncretism — the blending of Christianity with paganism. Twiss rejected both critiques. He argued that the gospel has always been translated into cultural forms — from the Greek philosophy of the early councils to the Gothic architecture of medieval Europe to the contemporary worship music of American evangelicalism — and that indigenous cultural forms deserve the same dignity and recognition as any other.</p>

<p>"Why is it," Twiss asked, "that an Italian painting of Jesus is considered sacred art, but a Lakota painting of Jesus is considered syncretism?" The question exposes the colonial double standard at the heart of Western Christianity's relationship with indigenous culture. European cultural forms are treated as neutral containers for the gospel. Indigenous cultural forms are treated as contaminated vessels that corrupt whatever they carry. This distinction has no theological basis. It is a cultural prejudice disguised as theological discernment.</p>

<p>Twiss died in 2013, but his legacy continues in the growing movement of indigenous Christian theology — a movement that refuses the colonial binary, claims the gospel on indigenous terms, and insists that the God who created all peoples created their cultures too. This movement is not a threat to Christianity. It is one of its most promising frontiers — a demonstration that the gospel is genuinely universal precisely because it is translatable into every particular culture, including the ones the church once tried to destroy.</p>

<p>The church that learns from indigenous spirituality is not departing from its own tradition. It is recovering dimensions of that tradition that the Enlightenment, colonialism, and Western cultural arrogance have obscured. The God of the Bible is a God of creation, of community, of the sacred in the ordinary. Indigenous traditions have preserved these truths. The church should receive them with the humility of a student, not the condescension of a teacher — and with the repentance of a community that once tried to silence the very voices that were saying what its own Scriptures have always said.</p>`
